# ==========================================
# Nix / direnv
# ==========================================

# The direnv cache. Regenerated by `direnv allow`; it holds symlinks into the
# nix store and a profile path named for this machine, so it is local state
# rather than anything to share. Its absence is not a broken checkout.
.direnv/


# ==========================================
# Reference documents
# ==========================================

# Design and asset reference PDFs, kept locally. They are large binaries that
# change, so committing them bloats the repository permanently for something
# that is read rather than built from. Scoped to docs/ rather than a blanket
# *.pdf so a PDF that genuinely belongs in the tree is not silently excluded.
docs/*.pdf

# EXCEPT THE THREE THAT ARE SPECIFICATIONS. Added 2026-10-15.
#
# The rule above is right for REFERENCE documents. It was wrong for these: they
# are the ORIGINAL SPECIFICATIONS of the system being built, and docs/assettypes01
# §5B shows how much they still hold that nothing has re-read — it specifies the
# check-out down to the manifest format, in more detail than anything written
# since it.
#
# SO THEY WERE KEPT OUT OF THE ONLY PLACE THAT OUTLIVES THIS MACHINE. A
# specification that exists on one laptop cannot be read from a clone, cannot be
# audited by anybody else, and is gone when the disk is.
#
# AND THEY COULD NOT BE AUDITED FROM HERE EITHER, WHICH IS HOW THIS WAS FOUND:
# there is no text extractor on this machine and the streams are font and image
# data, so a decompression yields noise. The only way to read them is to open
# them. 808 KB between them.
#
# ORDER MATTERS — a negation has to come after the pattern it undoes, which is
# why these sit here rather than in a section of their own.
!docs/OpenWorld Asset Library.pdf
!docs/OpenWorld Asset Repository & Development System.pdf
!docs/OpenWorld Asset System.pdf


# ==========================================
# Scratch
# ==========================================

# A WORKING directory, not a fixture directory. What lives here is whatever
# somebody is trying out right now — a folder of images to push through the
# library, a project to see what the upload makes of it.
#
# NOTHING IN THE PROJECT READS IT. Every check script builds its own scratch
# database and tests rules in memory; the tests that need a file make one rather
# than reading one from disk. So there is no test run that this could break by
# being absent.
#
# SO IT IS IGNORED, because `git add -A` picking up three test photographs is a
# mistake nobody notices until they are in the history.
#
# AND IF FIXTURES ARE EVER WANTED — files whose whole point is NOT changing —
# they belong somewhere tracked and named for what they are. `tmp` says
# disposable, and a fixture is the opposite.
tmp/

# The facts file an installation is configured with. It may be copied
# between machines, so it must never carry a credential — but it is still
# local configuration, not repository content.
deploy/site.env
